<onlyinclude>'''Silicon''' is a tetrahedrally bonded group-IV semiconductor with the diamond-cubic lattice. The indirect nature of its fundamental gap, controllable impurity ionization and technologically useful Si/SiO₂ interface jointly explain its central role in microelectronics; none of these properties alone guarantees device performance.</onlyinclude>

== Crystal and bands ==
The conventional cubic cell of diamond-structure Si contains eight atoms. With lattice constant a ≈ 5.431 Å at room temperature, its crystallographic atomic density is n = 8/a³. Converting a = 5.431 × 10⁻⁸ cm yields n ≈ 4.99 × 10²² atoms cm⁻³. This is a lattice-site density, not a free-carrier concentration. The indirect fundamental gap is about 1.12 eV at 300 K; the lowest optical transitions without phonon assistance occur at higher energies.<ref name="byu">[https://www.cleanroom.byu.edu/Semiconductor-Constants BYU Cleanroom, ''Semiconductor Constants''] (silicon lattice and band data).</ref>

== Carrier statistics and doping ==
For nondegenerate, thermally equilibrated silicon under the usual effective-density-of-states approximation,
: <math>n p = n_i^2</math>
where n and p are electron and hole densities, and n_i is the intrinsic concentration at the specified temperature. For fully ionized, uncompensated donors with N_D much larger than n_i, n ≈ N_D and p ≈ n_i²/N_D. The approximations fail or require modification under strong degeneracy, incomplete ionization, substantial compensation, or nonequilibrium illumination/injection. Dopants introduce electrically active levels; their incorporation, activation and diffusion are process-dependent.

== Oxidation and interface physics ==
Deal and Grove modeled thermal oxide thickness x through
: <math>x^2 + A x = B(t + \tau)</math>
with linear and parabolic kinetic coefficients A and B and a time offset τ representing initial oxide conditions.<ref name="dg">B. E. Deal and A. S. Grove, [https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1713945 ''General Relationship for the Thermal Oxidation of Silicon''], ''J. Appl. Phys.'' 36 (1965), 3770–3778.</ref>
At small x relative to A, the model predicts approximately linear growth; at large x relative to A, parabolic diffusion-limited growth. Coefficients depend on temperature, oxidant, pressure, orientation and process conditions; the equation must not be used as a universal recipe. Investigations of ultrathin films document deviations from the classical model.<ref name="thin">[https://doi.org/10.1155/2016/7545632 ''A Self-Consistent Model for Thermal Oxidation of Silicon at Low Oxide Thickness''] (2016).</ref>

== Device and process distinctions ==
Single-crystal bulk silicon is a substrate; doped regions and junctions are device structures; SiO₂ is an oxide; polysilicon and amorphous silicon have different order and transport. Silicon's indirect gap makes it comparatively inefficient for direct light emission. Interface traps, defects, strain, impurity concentrations and thermal history all affect measured behavior.

== Quantitative verification ==
With a = 5.431 × 10⁻⁸ cm: a³ ≈ 1.6017 × 10⁻²² cm³, hence 8/a³ ≈ 4.995 × 10²² cm⁻³. This illustrative calculation assumes an ideal conventional diamond-cubic cell, not measured density of a particular wafer.

